It's a long story.



I was minding my own business, that is watching Vlad's business, when I
ended up getting this job.  I found myself with my ususal assortment of
sharp pointy things, some leathers in place of my Jhereg colors and a small
dog.  This wasn't going to be easy.  There were assorted rooms wth a variety
of nasty things trying to kill me and my little dog too.  Stairs lead
downward and after a couple of these I discovered a level that had two.  One
lead to a dark place, and since I didn't have a light source, headed the
other way.  An oracle awaited a few floors down and the statues there
sounded hollow.  A wand that broke things let me open the hollow ones and
learn some of the local sorcery.  Some bugs interrupted my study at one
point, but my daggers made short work of them.  After that, came a puzzle
that involved a lot of strength training.  Boulders are a lot of work, but
the weight reducing bag at the end was worth the trouble.  The gold in the
end room would be useful as well.  I headed back up to that dark floor, one
of the local spells had been a light charm.  The dark floor turned out to be
the beginning of a decent sized mine.  The gnomes and dwarves were decidedly
unfriendly.  It took some convincing (mostly with thrown daggers) for them
to let me pass.  I found a small town, complete with guards and shops.
There was a temple dedicated to the guy who gave me the job, Kos.  Offered
him some of the remains of the less polite townspeople and he blessed some
water for me.  That allowed me to make my bag even lighter.  I also donated
a largish amount of gold to the priest, who assured me I would be harder to
hit.  Descending through the mine I encountered a dusty library at the
bottom.  My use of the Light charm eventually made me feel more confident in
the use of the local sorcery.  The spellbooks in the library yielded a
useful one that would allow me to discover the properties of the many
strange items I found along the way.  Also, I found a tough one that when
Identified promised to allow me to change one item into another.  A very
nasty creature with tentacles on it's head attacked me, making me feel dumb.
I managed to kill it with a zap of the wand that makes the bugs on the floor
move faster and many thrown daggers.  I returned to the town and locked
myself into the temple to identify the contents of my bag.  I discovered
that I had several scrolls that improved the sharpness of my weapons, some
more that improved the defense value of my armor.  I also had a pair of
gloves that would improve my manual dexterity.  I returned to the main
dungeon, finding a silver dagger which I decided to use for close in work,
returning my belt dagger to a throwing position.  I then enchanted the whole
stack as much as I felt safe. A few floors below the Oracle I found a Ghost
hovering over the body of a late Wizard who's name escapes me.  I've no way
of sending this poor fool to deathsgate, so I just dissipate the ghost and
look over his gear.  There's a Dagger named Magicbane, rather impressive
name.  Perhaps one of the Great Weapons of this place.  The silver dagger
was stashed for future use and this new one for up close and personal.  A
suit of dragonscale armor that reflected light was among the late Wizard's
effects as well.  A rather long worm was on this level.  Long?  Hell it took
up an entire room and extended into the corridor.  When it died I found a
single intact tooth.  It seemed to fit my hand quite well, and when
enchanted it became a very nasty knife.  So, I had my tooth/knife and the
Magicbane.



Some few levels later I found a spellbook that offered to allow me to dig
tunnels of my own.  It was an advanced spell, but I did practice.  I also
found a book that showed me where treasures were located.  Combining these I
discovered that several floors contained hidden vaults.  Digging new tunnels
I looted these planning on giving Kos' buddy the proceeds in hopes of
becoming harder to hit.  In one such Vault I stepped into a magical portal
that lead me to a massive fort.  It was a very tough fight, soldiers of all
kinds, and all of them seemed to notice me.  Maybe it was the alarm I set
off.  I found that my ability with the dagger was perfected during this
battle and began to use the knife on the soldiers to judge it's
effectiveness.  It cut deep, and I would be pleased to carry it throughout
this particular job.  After looting the fort's treasury (Damn that Creosus
guy was a paranoid.  Who puts landmines under piles of gold?)  I found
myself with so much gold that the bag was slowing me down.  Hell, it felt
like I had looted the Dragon Treasury.  After a trip to the town in the
mines I was much harder to hit, and Kos' priest was Very Rich.



